    "title": "Voyager 1: Complete Information & Live Data | TheSkyLive",
    "text": "Above the horizon. Rise: 21:34 (-1) | Tran: 04:40 | Set: 11:47 Alt: 50.07° Az: 194.52° Direction: South-South-West Voyager 1 is in the constellation of Ophiuchus, at a distance of 25,395,124,006.3 kilometers from Earth. The current Right Ascension is 17h 18m 02s and the Declination is +12° 17’ 36” (apparent coordinates). Finder Charts The sky charts below show, at different resolutions, the position of Voyager 1 in the sky. Click on each chart to access the interactive star map tool. Star map showing the position of Voyager 1 in the constellation of Ophiuchus on April 16, 2026. Field of view: 50x30 degrees. View interactive star map. Star map showing the position of Voyager 1 in the constellation of Ophiuchus on April 16, 2026. Field of view: 15x9 degrees. View in interactive star map. The following map uses photographic imagery from the Digitized Sky Survey (DSS) to realistically visualize the precise position of Voyager 1 against the stars in the background. The horizontal size of the map measures 60 arcminutes. Click on the image to access a more detailed full-screen view. Voyager 1 RA: 17h 16m 48s Dec: +12° 19’ 13” Simplified high-resolution deep sky chart showing the current position of Voyager 1. Field of view: 60 x 40 arcminutes. Go to the dedicated page Equatorial Coordinates Apparent Right Ascension17h 18m 02s J2000 Right Ascension17h 16m 48s Additional Information Rise and Set Times The following table lists today's rise, transit and set times of Voyager 1 from Greenwich, UK (all times relative to the local timezone Europe/London): Azimuth: 69.2° RISE 21:34 (-1) Max altitude: 50.8° TRANSIT 04:40 Azimuth: 290.8° SET 11:47 If you need to access this information frequently for your observations, you can create a simple customized Quick Access page, so that you can easily bookmark it in your browser favorites or add a shortcut to your mobile phones' home screen. Distance from Earth The distance of Voyager 1 from Earth is currently 25,395,124,006.3 kilometers, equivalent to 169.7559189007 Astronomical Units. Light takes 23h 31m 49.016s to travel from Voyager 1 and arrive to us. Distance Km25,395,124,006.3 Distance AU169.7559189007 Light Travel Time23h 31m 49.016s The following graph shows the distance of Voyager 1 from Earth as a function of time. In the chart the distance is measured in Astronomical Units and the data is sampled with an interval of 1 day. Chart of the distance of Voyager 1 from Earth in Astronomical Units (au) Note: The values in this chart may not be entirely accurate around the time of closest approach for objects passing very close to Earth at high speed (e.g., Near-Earth Objects). The value of the distance of Voyager 1 from Earth is also available as a real time updated value in the live position tracker. Closest Approach to Earth In the period between September 6, 1977 and December 30, 2099, the closest approach of Voyager 1 to Earth happened on Tuesday 6 September 1977 at a distance of 0.002576 Astronomical Units, or 385,420 kilometers: DateTuesday 6 September 1977 Distance Kilometers385,420 Note: the values for the closest approach are computed with a sampling interval of 1 day. Orbit Visualization The following chart is a 3D animated visualization of the orbit of Voyager 1 relative to the other major objects in the Solar System. 15 Days Ephemeris The following table lists the ephemerides of Voyager 1 computed for the past and next 7 days, with a 24 hours interval. Apparent celestial coordinates are provided. Date Right AscensionR.A. DeclinationDec. MagnitudeMag Constellation Apr 09 2026 17h 18m 07s +12° 16’ 17” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 10 2026 17h 18m 06s +12° 16’ 28” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 11 2026 17h 18m 06s +12° 16’ 39” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 12 2026 17h 18m 05s +12° 16’ 51” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 13 2026 17h 18m 04s +12° 17’ 02” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 14 2026 17h 18m 04s +12° 17’ 12” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 15 2026 17h 18m 03s +12° 17’ 23” N.A. Ophiuchus Apr 16 2026 17h 18m 02s +12° 17’ 34” N.A. Ophiuchus 